# Target C: matching versus canonical defect-currency fork

**Date:** 2026-10-02  
**Remote main rechecked before writing:** \`3c2c56be83ea637a560cc56bc5e9c0e49c72042e\`  
**Status:** proof-only exact reduction. Level I and C-II remain open.

## 1. Statement

Use the canonical notation for the full actual diameter graph

\[
H=(B,E_H),\qquad
q=|B|,\qquad
h=|E_H|,\qquad
d_H=q-h,
\]

and let

\[
\nu=\nu(H)
\]

be its maximum matching number.

Canonical Section 61 defines

\[
r_H:=2h-q=h-d_H
\]

and proves the exact currency identity

\[
\boxed{
r_H=x_B+Q+2s_Z,
}
\tag{1}
\]

where

- \(x_B=b-q\) is outer-face boundary-walk excess;
- \(Q\) is bounded-face degree excess above quadrilateral;
- \(s_Z\) is mixed-rank surplus.

The new observation is that canonical C-WOODALL bounds \(d_H\) directly by the
matching number.

### Theorem 1.1 -- matching/currency inequality

Every canonical post-ZF C-II source satisfies

\[
\boxed{
d_H\le\nu.
}
\tag{2}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
x_B+Q+2s_Z
=r_H
\ge h-\nu
\ge \lceil D\rceil-\nu.
}
\tag{3}
\]

This is finite and exact apart from the final canonical ZF inequality
\(h\ge\lceil D\rceil\).

## 2. Proof

Apply C-WOODALL.

### Cyclic branch

Here

\[
d_H=0,
\]

so (2) is immediate.

### Forest branch

Here \(H\) is a disjoint union of caterpillar trees and

\[
d_H
=
\#\{\text{diameter components}\}.
\]

Every component contains at least one edge. Choosing one edge from each component
gives a matching because different components are vertex-disjoint. Hence

\[
\nu\ge d_H.
\]

This proves (2).

Now

\[
r_H
=2h-q
=h-(q-h)
=h-d_H
\ge h-\nu.
\]

Canonical ZF gives \(h\ge\lceil D\rceil\), and the Section-61 identity (1) gives
(3). QED.

## 3. Global parameterized fork

For every fixed \(0<\epsilon<1\), every sufficiently large source satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\nu\ge\epsilon D
}
\tag{4}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
x_B+Q+2s_Z
\ge
(1-\epsilon)D-O(1).
}
\tag{5}
\]

The first branch contains a linear family of vertex-disjoint actual diameter edges.
By the diameter-thrackle property they are pairwise properly crossing, and their
endpoints have the exact offset matching order proved in the square-root braid note.

The second branch is entirely expressed in the canonical C-II currencies. Thus a
failure to produce a linear crossing braid is not a diffuse topology escape: it
forces almost a full diameter unit of outer-walk/face/rank defect.

## 4. Square-root hard regime

The current-main Woodall vertex-cover improvement proves

\[
\nu(H)
\ge
\left({1\over\sqrt{12\pi\sqrt3}}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D.
\tag{6}
\]

Suppose a subsequence remains on that hard scale,

\[
\nu(H)=O(\sqrt D).
\tag{7}
\]

Then (3) gives

\[
\boxed{
x_B+Q+2s_Z
\ge D-O(\sqrt D).
}
\tag{8}
\]

Hence at least one of the three weighted currencies is macroscopic:

\[
\boxed{
\max\{x_B,Q,2s_Z\}
\ge {D\over3}-O(\sqrt D).
}
\tag{9}
\]

Equivalently at least one of

\[
\boxed{
x_B\ge {D\over3}-O(\sqrt D),
}
\tag{10}
\]

\[
\boxed{
Q\ge {D\over3}-O(\sqrt D),
}
\tag{11}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
s_Z\ge {D\over6}-O(\sqrt D)
}
\tag{12}
\]

holds.

This upgrades the older universal nonmatching floor

\[
x_B+Q+2s_Z
=r_H
\ge (0.6099\ldots-o(1))D
\]

to an asymptotic coefficient \(1\) throughout the square-root matching regime.

## 5. Compatibility with the new leaf/core theorem

The current-main leaf/core reduction gives, under (7),

\[
D-O(\sqrt D)
\]

diameter leaves and only \(O(\sqrt D)\) nonleaf diameter carriers.

The new currency bound says this same two-level Woodall geometry must coexist with
(8). Therefore the hard branch has two simultaneous descriptions:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{l}
D-O(\sqrt D)\text{ pendant diameter terminals on }O(\sqrt D)\text{ carriers},\\[2mm]
x_B+Q+2s_Z\ge D-O(\sqrt D).
\end{array}
}
\tag{13}
\]

The leaf/core KKT theorem further says that normalized stress must either put
linear aggregate contact load at pendant terminals or concentrate a
\(D^{-1/2}\)-scale diameter multiplier on the small core.

This is substantially more rigid than either the topology or the currency identity
alone.

## 6. Branch-specific consequences

### 6.1 Small outer-walk excess

If

\[
x_B=o(D),
\]

then in the square-root hard regime

\[
\boxed{
Q+2s_Z\ge D-o(D).
}
\tag{14}
\]

Thus either bounded nonquadrilateral-face excess is linear with coefficient bounded
away from zero, or the mixed-row surplus is linear.

### 6.2 Small face excess

If

\[
Q=o(D),
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
x_B+2s_Z\ge D-o(D).
}
\tag{15}
\]

### 6.3 Low mixed surplus

If

\[
s_Z=o(D),
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
x_B+Q\ge D-o(D).
}
\tag{16}
\]

So no two of the three defect mechanisms can simultaneously be negligible in the
hard matching regime.

## 7. Remaining obstruction

The theorem does not provide an upper bound on the right side of (1); current global
geometry permits linear outer-walk, face, and rank defect. The gain is that the
square-root matching endpoint has now been transported exactly into the canonical
currency system.

The next high-value attack is therefore branch-specific rather than another matching
estimate:

1. **large \(x_B\):** use the \(D-O(\sqrt D)\) pendant diameter terminals against the
   outer-walk detour and contact-leaf charge already proved on current main;
2. **large \(Q\):** combine linear nonquadrilateral-face degree excess with the
   near-disk ball-hull / inactive-interface geometry;
3. **large \(s_Z\):** use the linear mixed-rank surplus to force a large omitted-row
   face or a simultaneous finite release, rather than merely selecting an extreme
   KKT stress.

The macroscopic inactive-container branch remains separate.

**Disposition:** exact proof-only reduction. No computation, numerical search,
verifier execution, or CI output is used.
